      "content": "The \"scientific\" justifications were heavily influenced by flawed eugenics movements, which claimed that certain races were biologically inferior and less capable of self-governance. Proponents often cited outdated anthropological theories suggesting inherent differences in intelligence or temperament between races. Sociologically, they argued that integration would lead to social friction and the dilution of superior racial stock, framing separation as a necessary form of \"social hygiene.\" These ideas were presented as objective facts, despite being entirely baseless prejudice.",
