    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The logic of \"social hygiene\" was built on the idea of a national body needing to remain pure and strong, much like a physical body needs to be kept healthy. They believed that mixing races would introduce \"weakness\" or \"degeneracy\" into the national character, slowing down or reversing the supposed natural process of social evolution. Therefore, segregation was framed not as discrimination, but as a necessary public health measure—a protective barrier to ensure the dominant culture could continue to thrive and evolve according to its perceived superior trajectory. This allowed them to present their prejudice as a rational, almost medical, imperative for national survival.",
      "source": "target",
